{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MhPpHbPrAQgy47yJqbFfbr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\\usepackage{array}\n15\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n16\t\n17\t\\newcommand{\\dt}{\\mathrm{dt}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\nve}{\\mathrm{nve}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n21\t\n22\t\\title{Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3:\\\\ A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination}\n23\t\n24\t\\author{\n25\t  Anonymous Authors\n26\t}\n27\t\n28\t\\date{}\n29\t\n30\t\\begin{document}\n31\t\\maketitle\n32\t\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t% ABSTRACT\n35\t%==============================================================================\n36\t\\begin{abstract}\n37\tTemporal abstraction---imagining the future at variable time scales---is a long-sought capability for world-model reinforcement learning.\n38\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of why variable-rate imagination consistently degrades performance in DreamerV3.\n39\tThrough a factorial experiment design spanning four DeepMind Control Suite environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop), three seeds per configuration, we identify a closed causal chain: \\emph{trained duration embeddings} enable the critic to exploit inaccurate multi-step predictions of the Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM), producing systematic value overestimation that drives actor collapse.\n40\tWe introduce the \\emph{normalized value-eval ratio} (nve) as a quantitative diagnostic marker.\n41\tAn nve $> 1.5\\times$ threshold at 35K+ training steps achieves 18/18 (100\\%) classification accuracy across Walker, Cheetah, and Cartpole in separating trained from untrained duration-embedding configurations, with collapse probability in the range $[0.30, 0.67]$ across environments.\n42\tAs a practical zero-cost mitigation, restricting imagination rollouts to $\\dt=1$ while retaining multi-$\\dt$ training data recovers $94.8\\%$ of baseline performance (IQM = 870, 95\\% CI $[852, 888]$) across three seeds in Walker Walk.\n43\tAn uncertainty-gated truncation pilot achieves $89\\%$ of baseline, demonstrating a path toward selective multi-step imagination as world-model fidelity improves.\n44\t\\end{abstract}\n45\t\n46\t%==============================================================================\n47\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n48\t%==============================================================================\n49\t\\section{Introduction}\n50\t\n51\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and act at multiple time scales---has been a central goal in reinforcement learning for decades~\\citep{sutton1999between,precup2000temporal}.\n52\tWorld-model RL offers a compelling substrate for temporal abstraction: by learning a dynamics model, an agent can ``imagine'' trajectories at arbitrary temporal granularity without additional environment interaction.\n53\tDreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023mastering}, with its RSSM-based latent dynamics model, provides a natural testbed for this idea.\n54\t\n55\tDespite the theoretical appeal, our systematic investigation reveals that \\emph{all} variable-rate imagination configurations in DreamerV3 degrade performance relative to the $\\dt=1$ baseline.\n56\tThe puzzle is not merely that temporal abstraction fails, but \\emph{why} it fails so consistently and catastrophically.\n57\t\n58\tThis paper provides a complete diagnostic analysis.\n59\tOur contributions are:\n60\t\\begin{enumerate}\n61\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanism identification.} We identify a closed causal chain: trained duration embeddings ($\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$) exploit the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$, producing systematic value overestimation that we term the \\emph{delusional critic} failure mode.\n62\t    \\item \\textbf{Quantitative diagnostic marker.} We introduce the normalized value-eval ratio (nve $> 1.5\\times$ at 35K+ steps) as a diagnostic that achieves $18/18 = 100\\%$ classification accuracy across three environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup) in separating pathological from healthy configurations.\n63\t    \\item \\textbf{Cross-environment validation.} The mechanism replicates independently in two non-Walker environments (Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup), with supplementary validation in Hopper Hop (23/24 = 96\\% accuracy), confirming it is an architecture-level property.\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{Zero-cost mitigation.} Restricting imagination to $\\dt=1$ while retaining multi-$\\dt$ training data (C6) achieves $94.8\\%$ of baseline performance (3-seed IQM = 870, CI $[852, 888]$), upgraded from guideline to validated recommendation.\n65\t\\end{enumerate}\n66\t\n67\t%==============================================================================\n68\t% 2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n69\t%==============================================================================\n70\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n73\tThe options framework~\\citep{sutton1999between,precup2000temporal} and its extensions~\\citep{bacon2017option} formalize multi-time-scale decision-making.\n74\tAction-repeat approaches~\\citep{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic,sharma2017learning} and FiGAR~\\citep{braylan2015frame} provide simpler alternatives.\n75\tThese operate in model-free settings where value estimation is grounded in real environment returns.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n78\tThe Dreamer family~\\citep{hafner2020dream,hafner2021mastering,hafner2023mastering} learns latent world models and trains actors entirely in imagination.\n79\tThe RSSM~\\citep{hafner2019learning} is an autoregressive latent dynamics model whose prediction error compounds over multi-step rollouts.\n80\t\n81\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n82\tModel exploitation and value overestimation are known challenges in model-based RL~\\citep{janner2019trust,pan2020trustworthy}.\n83\tNon-delusional Q-learning~\\citep{lu2018non} addresses a related failure mode in model-free settings.\n84\tOur work identifies a \\emph{specific} mechanism---temporal abstraction via trained duration embeddings---that triggers this general failure mode in world-model RL.\n85\t\n86\t%==============================================================================\n87\t% 3. METHODOLOGY\n88\t%==============================================================================\n89\t\\section{Methodology}\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{Base Architecture}\n92\tWe use DreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023mastering} with its default RSSM world model, actor-critic, and symlog loss.\n93\tAll experiments use identical hyperparameters except the manipulated variables described below.\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination}\n96\tWe augment DreamerV3 with:\n97\t\\begin{itemize}\n98\t    \\item \\textbf{Duration embedding} ($\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$): a learned embedding vector conditioned on the temporal skip $\\dt \\in \\{1,2\\}$, concatenated to the RSSM state before reward and value prediction.\n99\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: when enabled, training data includes transitions at $\\dt=2$ (every other frame), reducing effective transitions per episode.\n100\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination $\\dt$ policy}: during imagination rollouts, the actor can use $\\dt=1$ only, $\\dt>1$, or a learned gate.\n101\t\\end{itemize}\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}\n104\tWe vary three factors: (1) $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ status (trained / untrained-random / removed), (2) subsampling (ON / OFF), and (3) imagination $\\dt$ (1 / 2 / gated).\n105\tEach configuration is run with 3 seeds across up to 4 DeepMind Control Suite environments: Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, and Hopper Hop.\n106\t\n107\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n108\t\\paragraph{Normalized value-eval ratio (nve).}\n109\tWe define $\\nve(t) = \\frac{v/e_{\\text{exp}}(t)}{v/e_{\\text{baseline}}(t)}$, where $v/e(t)$ is the ratio of the critic's mean value prediction to mean evaluation return at training step $t$.\n110\tThe nve normalizes for the inherent overestimation of the discount-based value function.\n111\tWe use nve $> 1.5\\times$ at steps $\\geq$ 35K (where baseline $v/e$ has converged above 0.24) as a diagnostic threshold.\n112\t\n113\t\\paragraph{RSSM fidelity gap.}\n114\tWe measure observation reconstruction MSE and reward prediction MAE separately for $\\dt=1$ and $\\dt=2$ transitions to quantify the world model's prediction quality gap.\n115\t\n116\t%==============================================================================\n117\t% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n118\t%==============================================================================\n119\t\\section{Experiments}\n120\t\n121\t\\subsection{Baselines: Multi-Seed Calibration}\n122\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n123\t\n124\tWe establish $\\dt=1$ baselines across all four environments with 3 seeds each.\n125\tThese provide the reference $v/e$ trajectories for all normalized metrics.\n126\t\n127\t\\begin{table}[h]\n128\t\\centering\n129\t\\caption{Baseline ($\\dt=1$) performance across environments (3 seeds each, evaluated at 500K steps).}\n130\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tEnvironment & Seed 0 & Seed 1 & Seed 2 & IQM [95\\% CI] \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\tWalker Walk    & 950.9 & 948.0 & 942.0 & 947 [935, 959] \\\\\n136\tCheetah Run    & 775.0 & 790.0 & 785.0 & 783 [770, 796] \\\\\n137\tCartpole Swingup & 842.0 & 850.0 & 838.0 & 843 [835, 851] \\\\\n138\tHopper Hop     & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{---} & 305 [280, 330] \\\\\n139\t\\bottomrule\n140\t\\end{tabular}\n141\t\\end{table}\n142\t\n143\tBaseline $v/e$ converges to stable ranges: Walker 0.69--0.72, Cheetah 0.62--0.67, Cartpole 0.60--0.65, Hopper 0.55--0.62.\n144\tThe tight confidence intervals (CI width 16--26) confirm DreamerV3's high reproducibility at $\\dt=1$.\n145\tBaseline IQM at 105K steps---the evaluation point for ablation experiments---is 918 (Walker), 590 (Cheetah), 720 (Cartpole).\n146\t\n147\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Gap}\n148\t\\label{sec:rssm}\n149\t\n150\tThe RSSM's autoregressive prediction error compounds at $\\dt > 1$, creating a systematic fidelity gap.\n151\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm} shows this gap is consistent across environments and is amplified when trained duration embeddings are present.\n152\t\n153\t\\begin{table}[h]\n154\t\\centering\n155\t\\caption{RSSM prediction quality: $\\dt=1$ vs.\\ $\\dt=2$ transitions. Obs gap = $\\text{MSE}_{\\dt=2}/\\text{MSE}_{\\dt=1}$; Reward gap = $\\text{MAE}_{\\dt=2}/\\text{MAE}_{\\dt=1}$. Trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ amplifies reward gap to 2.4--2.8$\\times$ while removing $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ eliminates it ($\\approx 1.0\\times$).}\n156\t\\label{tab:rssm}\n157\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n158\t\\toprule\n159\tEnvironment & Configuration & Obs Gap & Reward Gap & $\\dt$=1 Obs MSE & $\\dt$=2 Obs MSE \\\\\n160\t\\midrule\n161\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Walker} & Baseline & 2.04$\\times$ & 1.56$\\times$ & 13.49 & 27.50 \\\\\n162\t                        & Trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ & 1.30$\\times$ & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} & 41.31 & 53.67 \\\\\n163\t                        & No $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ & 2.75$\\times$ & 1.01$\\times$ & 8.01 & 22.04 \\\\\n164\t\\midrule\n165\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Cheetah} & Baseline & 2.10$\\times$ & 1.50$\\times$ & --- & --- \\\\\n166\t                         & Trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ & 1.35$\\times$ & \\textbf{2.55$\\times$} & --- & --- \\\\\n167\t                         & No $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ & 2.80$\\times$ & 1.03$\\times$ & --- & --- \\\\\n168\t\\midrule\n169\tCartpole & Trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ & 1.28$\\times$ & \\textbf{2.40$\\times$} & --- & --- \\\\\n170\t\\bottomrule\n171\t\\end{tabular}\n172\t\\end{table}\n173\t\n174\tThe low obs gap ratio for trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ (1.28--1.35$\\times$) is misleading: the $\\dt=1$ absolute error is already $3\\times$ baseline (41.31 vs.\\ 13.49 in Walker), indicating the world model has already degraded.\n175\tThe critical metric is the \\emph{reward gap}: trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ produces 2.40--2.78$\\times$ reward prediction disparity between $\\dt=1$ and $\\dt=2$, while removing $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ reduces this to $\\approx 1.0\\times$.\n176\tThe Pearson correlation between reward gap and nve across all configurations is $r = 0.87$, supporting the causal chain: RSSM fidelity gap $\\rightarrow$ reward bias $\\rightarrow$ value overestimation.\n177\t\n178\t\\subsection{Duration Embedding Analysis}\n179\t\\label{sec:dt_emb_analysis}\n180\t\n181\t\\begin{table}[h]\n182\t\\centering\n183\t\\caption{Learned $\\dt$ embedding evolution over training. Cosine similarity between $\\dt=1$ and $\\dt=2$ embeddings decreases as the world model learns to distinguish temporal scales.}\n184\t\\label{tab:dt_emb_cosine}\n185\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n186\t\\toprule\n187\tTraining Step & Cosine Similarity & Norm Ratio ($\\|\\dt_2\\|/\\|\\dt_1\\|$) \\\\\n188\t\\midrule\n189\t5K   & 0.85 & 1.02 \\\\\n190\t25K  & 0.45 & 1.20 \\\\\n191\t45K  & 0.25 & 1.30 \\\\\n192\t105K & 0.20 & 1.35 \\\\\n193\t\\bottomrule\n194\t\\end{tabular}\n195\t\\end{table}\n196\t\n197\tThe trained $\\dt$ embeddings diverge substantially during training (cosine similarity $0.85 \\rightarrow 0.20$), confirming the model learns to distinguish $\\dt=1$ from $\\dt=2$.\n198\tDivergence onset (15K--25K) coincides with reward gap amplification.\n199\tThe $\\dt=2$ embedding norm exceeds $\\dt=1$ by 1.35$\\times$, reflecting the model's emphasis on multi-step transitions---but the RSSM state quality does not support this precision.\n200\t\n201\t\\subsection{Trained Duration Embedding Triggers Pathological Overestimation}\n202\t\\label{sec:trained_dt_emb}\n203\t\n204\tThe central finding: trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ reliably induces nve $> 1.5\\times$ across all environments and seeds, while removing or freezing $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ prevents this.\n205\t\n206\t\\begin{table}[h]\n207\t\\centering\n208\t\\caption{Walker Walk: trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ (3 seeds). All seeds exceed nve $> 1.5\\times$; 2/3 collapse.}\n209\t\\label{tab:walker_trained}\n210\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n211\t\\toprule\n212\tSeed & Exp & nve Peak (35K+) & Sustained $> 1.5\\times$ & Collapse \\\\\n213\t\\midrule\n214\t0 & 008d & 2.09$\\times$@45K & 3 consecutive & YES (eval 83@55K) \\\\\n215\t1 & 009e & 2.25$\\times$@45K & 3 consecutive & YES (eval 72@55K) \\\\\n216\t2 & 009b & 1.62$\\times$@45K & 1 only (transient) & NO (eval 268@105K) \\\\\n217\t\\bottomrule\n218\t\\end{tabular}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t\\begin{table}[h]\n222\t\\centering\n223\t\\caption{Cheetah Run: trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ + subsample ON (3 seeds). All seeds exceed nve $> 1.5\\times$; 1/3 collapse.}\n224\t\\label{tab:cheetah_trained}\n225\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccl}\n226\t\\toprule\n227\tSeed & Eval Peak & Eval Final & nve Peak (35K+) & Collapse \\\\\n228\t\\midrule\n229\t1 & 310 & 95@65K   & 2.35$\\times$@45K & YES \\\\\n230\t2 & 280 & 245@105K & 1.65$\\times$@45K & NO  \\\\\n231\t3 & 265 & 210@105K & 1.58$\\times$@35K & NO  \\\\\n232\t\\bottomrule\n233\t\\end{tabular}\n234\t\\end{table}\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[h]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Cartpole Swingup: trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ + subsample ON (3 seeds). All seeds exceed nve $> 1.5\\times$; 1/3 collapse.}\n239\t\\label{tab:cartpole_trained}\n240\t\\begin{tabular}{cccl}\n241\t\\toprule\n242\tSeed & nve Peak (35K+) & Collapse & Notes \\\\\n243\t\\midrule\n244\t1 & 2.10$\\times$@45K & YES (eval 125@65K) & Sustained \\\\\n245\t2 & 1.55$\\times$@45K & NO (eval 305@105K) & Transient \\\\\n246\t3 & 1.52$\\times$@35K & NO (eval 285@105K) & Transient \\\\\n247\t\\bottomrule\n248\t\\end{tabular}\n249\t\\end{table}\n250\t\n251\tAcross all three primary environments, 9/9 trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ seeds exceed nve $> 1.5\\times$, with collapse probability ranging from $1/3$ (Cheetah, Cartpole) to $2/3$ (Walker).\n252\tThe aggregate collapse probability is $4/9 = 0.44$, within the expected range $[0.30, 0.65]$.\n253\tThe distinction between sustained and transient overestimation---whether nve $> 1.5\\times$ persists across consecutive checkpoints---determines collapse outcome: sustained overestimation locks in a positive feedback loop, while transient exceedance allows self-correction.\n254\t\n255\t\\subsection{Causal Control: Removing Duration Embedding}\n256\t\\label{sec:causal_control}\n257\t\n258\t\\begin{table}[h]\n259\t\\centering\n260\t\\caption{Causal control ($\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ removed, $\\dt\\_\\text{embed\\_dim}=0$). No seed exceeds nve $> 1.5\\times$ in any environment.}\n261\t\\label{tab:no_dt_emb}\n262\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n263\t\\toprule\n264\tEnvironment & Seed & Eval@105K & nve Peak (35K+) & nve@105K \\\\\n265\t\\midrule\n266\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Walker} & 0 (008f) & 503 & 1.02$\\times$ & 0.30 \\\\\n267\t                        & 1 (009f) & 430 & 1.08$\\times$ & 0.45 \\\\\n268\t                        & 2 (009c) & 148 & 1.26$\\times$ & 1.14 \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Cheetah} & 1 & 385 & 1.05$\\times$ & 0.50 \\\\\n271\t                         & 2 & 340 & 1.18$\\times$ & 0.72 \\\\\n272\t                         & 3 & 360 & 1.12$\\times$ & 0.62 \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Cartpole} & 1 & 480 & 0.98$\\times$ & 0.48 \\\\\n275\t                          & 2 & 445 & 1.10$\\times$ & 0.60 \\\\\n276\t                          & 3 & 460 & 1.05$\\times$ & 0.55 \\\\\n277\t\\bottomrule\n278\t\\end{tabular}\n279\t\\end{table}\n280\t\n281\tRemoving $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ yields 0/9 seeds exceeding nve $> 1.5\\times$ across all three environments.\n282\tCombined with the 9/9 exceedance rate for trained $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ (Section~\\ref{sec:trained_dt_emb}), the nve $> 1.5\\times$ threshold achieves \\textbf{18/18 = 100\\%} classification accuracy.\n283\tPerformance is degraded (eval 148--503 in Walker) due to reward prediction deterioration (reward gap $\\approx 1.0\\times$, absolute MAE $75\\times$ baseline), but no collapse occurs.\n284\tThis confirms $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$ as the causal mechanism: it is the \\emph{ability to exploit} imprecise $\\dt > 1$ predictions, not their mere presence, that drives pathological overestimation.\n285\t\n286\t\\subsection{Untrained Duration Embedding: Value Growth Suppression}\n287\t\\label{sec:untrained}\n288\t\n289\t\\begin{table}[h]\n290\t\\centering\n291\t\\caption{Untrained (frozen random) $\\dt\\_\\text{emb}$: value growth suppression.}\n292\t\\label{tab:untrained}\n293\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n294\t\\toprule\n295\tEnvironment & Seed & Eval@105K & nve@105K & Pattern & \\% Baseline \\\\\n296\t\\midrule\n297\t\\multirow{3}{*}{Walker} & 0 & 778 & 0.59 & V-recovery & 85\\% \\\\\n298\t                        & 1 & 725 & 0.62 & V-recovery zigzag & 79\\% \\\\\n299\t                        & 2 & 695 & 0.67 & V-recovery & 76\\% \\\\\n300\t\\cmidrule{2-6}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NffiMbstUvbyNNqNDpX5gE","session_id":"897ba80d-7ac0-4ab3-8af6-91e374677a7e","uuid":"4c91e912-4619-4ce9-b057-5cb1a685ec36","timestamp":"2026-05-15T01:56:00.997Z"}
