{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DDvtTLKdSVnWwhn65WnVFj","type":"tool_result","content":"499\tCollapse is a stochastic consequence (probability 0.44), determined by whether overestimation persists long enough to lock in the positive feedback loop.\n500\tRestricting imagination to $\\dt=1$ provides a zero-cost mitigation recovering 94.8\\% of baseline performance.\n501\tThese findings provide actionable guidance for practitioners and identify RSSM prediction quality at $\\dt > 1$ as the key bottleneck for temporal abstraction in world-model RL.\n502\t\n503\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n504\tOur analysis is scoped to DreamerV3 and four DMC environments.\n505\tWhile the mechanism is architecture-level (RSSM fidelity gap), generalization to other world-model architectures (\\eg, Transformer-based) requires further investigation.\n506\tThe C6 mitigation is validated only for Walker Walk (3 seeds); cross-environment validation is planned.\n507\t\n508\t\\paragraph{Future work.}\n509\tThe uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot motivates adaptive multi-step imagination conditioned on world-model confidence.\n510\tImproving RSSM prediction quality at $\\dt > 1$---\\eg, through multi-step training losses or uncertainty-aware dynamics models---would address the root cause rather than the symptom.\n511\t\n512\t%==============================================================================\n513\t% REFERENCES\n514\t%==============================================================================\n515\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Bacon et~al.(2017)]{bacon2017option}\n520\tP.-L.~Bacon, J.~Harb, and D.~Precup.\n521\t\\newblock The option-critic architecture.\n522\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2017.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Braylan et~al.(2015)]{braylan2015frame}\n525\tA.~Braylan, M.~Hollenbeck, E.~Meyerson, and R.~Miikkulainen.\n526\t\\newblock Frame skip is a powerful parameter for learning to play {A}tari.\n527\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI Workshop}, 2015.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2019)]{hafner2019learning}\n530\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, I.~Fischer, R.~Villegas, D.~Ha, H.~Lee, and J.~Davidson.\n531\t\\newblock Learning latent dynamics for planning from pixels.\n532\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2020)]{hafner2020dream}\n535\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, J.~Ba, and M.~Norouzi.\n536\t\\newblock Dream to control: Learning behaviors by latent imagination.\n537\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2021)]{hafner2021mastering}\n540\tD.~Hafner, T.~Lillicrap, M.~Norouzi, and J.~Ba.\n541\t\\newblock Mastering {A}tari with discrete world models.\n542\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem[Hafner et~al.(2023)]{hafner2023mastering}\n545\tD.~Hafner, J.~Pasukonis, J.~Ba, and T.~Lillicrap.\n546\t\\newblock Mastering diverse domains through world models.\n547\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2301.04104}, 2023.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem[Henderson et~al.(2018)]{henderson2018deep}\n550\tP.~Henderson, R.~Islam, P.~Bachman, J.~Pineau, D.~Precup, and D.~Meger.\n551\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning that matters.\n552\t\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2018.\n553\t\n554\t\\bibitem[Janner et~al.(2019)]{janner2019trust}\n555\tM.~Janner, J.~Fu, M.~Zhang, and S.~Levine.\n556\t\\newblock When to trust your model: Model-based policy optimization.\n557\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2019.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem[Lakshminarayanan et~al.(2017)]{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}\n560\tA.~S.~Lakshminarayanan, S.~Sharma, and B.~Ravindran.\n561\t\\newblock Dynamic frame skip deep {Q}-network.\n562\t\\newblock In \\emph{IJCAI Workshop}, 2017.\n563\t\n564\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2018)]{lu2018non}\n565\tT.~Lu, D.~Schuurmans, and C.~Boutilier.\n566\t\\newblock Non-delusional {Q}-learning and value-iteration.\n567\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2018.\n568\t\n569\t\\bibitem[Nikishin et~al.(2022)]{nikishin2022primacy}\n570\tE.~Nikishin, M.~Schwarzer, P.~D'Oro, P.-L.~Bacon, and A.~Courville.\n571\t\\newblock The primacy bias in deep reinforcement learning.\n572\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2022.\n573\t\n574\t\\bibitem[Pan et~al.(2020)]{pan2020trustworthy}\n575\tF.~Pan, J.~He, D.~Huo, and A.~White.\n576\t\\newblock Trustworthy policy optimization in model-based reinforcement learning.\n577\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2020.\n578\t\n579\t\\bibitem[Precup(2000)]{precup2000temporal}\n580\tD.~Precup.\n581\t\\newblock Temporal abstraction in reinforcement learning.\n582\t\\newblock PhD thesis, University of Massachusetts Amherst, 2000.\n583\t\n584\t\\bibitem[Sharma et~al.(2017)]{sharma2017learning}\n585\tS.~Sharma, A.~S.~Lakshminarayanan, and B.~Ravindran.\n586\t\\newblock Learning to repeat: Fine grained action repetition for deep reinforcement learning.\n587\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2017.\n588\t\n589\t\\bibitem[Sutton et~al.(1999)]{sutton1999between}\n590\tR.~S.~Sutton, D.~Precup, and S.~Singh.\n591\t\\newblock Between {MDP}s and semi-{MDP}s: A framework for temporal abstraction in reinforcement learning.\n592\t\\newblock \\emph{Artificial Intelligence}, 112(1-2):181--211, 1999.\n593\t\n594\t\\bibitem[Agarwal et~al.(2021)]{agarwal2021deep}\n595\tR.~Agarwal, M.~Schwarzer, P.~S.~Castro, A.~C.~Courville, and M.~G.~Bellemare.\n596\t\\newblock Deep reinforcement learning at the edge of the statistical precipice.\n597\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2021.\n598\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KvMFu7t7wxihwPseYjFE5w","session_id":"897ba80d-7ac0-4ab3-8af6-91e374677a7e","uuid":"c9889053-5fb2-4c39-8c33-e90448b68609","timestamp":"2026-05-15T01:56:58.542Z"}
